WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.000
CounterLab is for ML learners and instructors. This fixed sample changes one variable—who is new—and a 98.5% score becomes 59.4%. No model call produced these values.

00:12.000 --> 00:27.000
CounterLab begins with the Question. It treats notebook evidence as untrusted data, never runs a cell, and sends no raw rows, notebook file, or local path.

00:27.000 --> 00:43.000
GPT-5.6 proposes these competing explanations from only the approved sanitized packet. It cannot run the notebook or decide the result. The learner seals an immutable Prediction before any experimental value appears.

00:43.000 --> 00:58.000
Runtime Codex proposes only bounded operation IDs. The fixed scorer selects the test, and the frozen verifier decides whether the fixed kernel may release evidence.

00:58.000 --> 01:18.000
The numbers come from the fixed kernel. GPT-5.6 and Codex may explain or propose, but neither computes or verifies them.

01:18.000 --> 01:34.000
The learner explores already verified cells; the browser and the model calculate nothing new.

01:34.000 --> 01:52.000
No model grades the sentence. The fixed evaluator checks transfer on a different surface.

01:52.000 --> 02:10.000
Repair stays locked until deterministic transfer passes, and CounterLab patches only a copy.

02:10.000 --> 02:30.000
The Reasoning Diff is for the learner. The Proof Capsule binds the machine-readable evidence, versions, limitations, and event chain.

02:30.000 --> 02:45.000
Codex also accelerated the build through repository exploration, tests, and bounded repairs; humans fixed the scope and authority rules. Learner-impact status is NO_DATA, so we claim no measured learning gain. Chatbots explain. CounterLab lets reality answer.
